# File: specs/bug-fix-aggregate-status-truncation-of-cumulative-specs/feature.gherkin
#
# Generated from: specs/bug-fix-aggregate-status-truncation-of-cumulative-specs/requirements.md
# Issue: #169
# Type: Defect regression

@regression
Feature: Aggregate status truncates resolver-valid cumulative specs
  Aggregate status previously supplied a 65,536-byte reader to a scope resolver
  whose canonical Markdown contract permits complete documents up to 262,144 bytes.
  The scope-specific adapter now reads the same complete bounded content as direct inspection.

  @SCN001 @regression
  Scenario: Aggregate status reads a valid mapped identifier after 65,536 bytes
    Given a cumulative spec document larger than 65,536 bytes and no larger than 262,144 bytes
    And a manifest-owned task identifier occurs after byte 65,536
    When direct scope inspection and aggregate lifecycle status inspect the active issue
    Then both results include the late task in the same inventory and delivery slice
    And both scope results are scoped with no gaps

  @SCN002 @regression
  Scenario: Unrelated reader bounds and the resolver maximum remain unchanged
    Given status discovery and verification reads retain their existing explicit or default bounds
    When the aggregate scope adapter uses the resolver-specific Markdown limit
    Then unrelated status reads do not inherit the broader scope limit
    And a scope document above 262,144 bytes fails closed with the existing size diagnostic

  @SCN003 @regression
  Scenario: Correct scope parity advances the lifecycle beyond specification repair
    Given a resolver-valid cumulative fixture maps a late task to the active issue
    When aggregate status infers the lifecycle from the complete scope result
    Then the scope status matches direct inspection as scoped
    And the next action is $nmg-sdlc:write-code for the active issue
    And the next action is not $nmg-sdlc:write-spec
